#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "8cb590c5-586d-56a1-af70-d693ea055419" :question/math-academy-id "e-14996" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) =\\sin{t} \\,\\mathbf i -\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖i+j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)}{1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/id #uuid "2f92f568-0bf5-58bd-87ce-e6be8d05fe69" :question/math-academy-id "e-14997" :question/problem "Given  the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sqrt 3 t , \\:  \\cos t, \\: \\sin{t}\\right\\rangle,$ find the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve." :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f5e6498b-73f9-5760-81bb-14e9acc495f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14998" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) = \\sin{t} \\cdot \\mathbf q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-2\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07d7c8f4-5bc4-5c4a-8d29-3a75adddbaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259947} {:answer/id #uuid "393deb48-4b73-5a3a-b922-fa3eaa3e1bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-\\sqrt{3},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260384} {:answer/id #uuid "4f528ad2-861d-5736-bcbd-43986b22035b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260553} {:answer/id #uuid "99ef2b01-b2ae-523d-becc-16fad588d0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261209} {:answer/id #uuid "fff87119-33b5-5cfc-868b-95cd1174c3db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ef2b01-b2ae-523d-becc-16fad588d0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261209} :answer-field/id #uuid "afaf0fc7-b5cc-52f2-ae01-dc4ffbaccce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0386dac5-9403-597b-be20-d67f4b02e9a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127335" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖ & =‖⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}((-\\sqrt{3})(-1)+(1)(-\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e39ea1-4cba-57a5-ab90-7a89782cb8d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442564} {:answer/id #uuid "34323a95-3364-5d8b-ae6b-9b380e0bf2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442569} {:answer/id #uuid "37ad2308-55f9-592a-86af-f156397fae09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{2},-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442570} {:answer/id #uuid "77fd4f80-1bca-57c5-b06d-9f6931a1ae23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442582} {:answer/id #uuid "b691e5f8-bc54-56bd-ba8e-fb29b5a7d2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b691e5f8-bc54-56bd-ba8e-fb29b5a7d2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442593} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b8a9865-39bf-545e-aeef-35a531125f5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f08885d9-557e-5e55-b967-a7cb452a9e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127379" :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore, $P(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,t⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}})⟨2,\\,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨2,\\,t⟩) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}⟨2,\\,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}(-t⟨2,t⟩+({t}^{2}+4)⟨0,1⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{((1)^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}⟨-2(1),\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}⟨-2,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}‖⟨-1,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2(-1)+1(2)) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d17be68-2a76-543b-a622-b3654ac5d053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259999} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9b5b82-03b1-5f24-9bf4-f4828136cfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260836} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6ba1a1-0008-519d-b2c5-d78240665f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260942} {:answer/id #uuid "bb19420d-b4ae-5cb5-8764-2269913eca36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261520} {:answer/id #uuid "e672199c-f760-5aca-81de-0a63ad98cdec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a6ba1a1-0008-519d-b2c5-d78240665f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260942} :answer-field/id #uuid "35b86093-86ba-5f1c-a960-515ef80708a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc8e8967-4072-5705-809d-5e532a5002c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-127595" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=2,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(2)=⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(2)=⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(1+4t)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{2}{(1+4(2))^{3/2}}\\cdot q(2)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4(2)}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}(-2⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩+9⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}⟨-2,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{54}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{54}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{54}\\cdot \\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{54}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{18})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2)\\cdot N(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}q(2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(1\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2})+2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(-2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b10125-03d9-569f-b489-2af1cd51651f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442566} {:answer/id #uuid "625e5e39-ae98-52ef-bfcc-de3e0bace052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{2},1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442579} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ec4419-91f6-5d58-9711-3de15ef93020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442596} {:answer/id #uuid "f33db979-e5fc-564b-9e72-04e2ed505855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442598} {:answer/id #uuid "f911a805-3f13-582e-9b9e-8ec61aec91fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0ec4419-91f6-5d58-9711-3de15ef93020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442596} :answer-field/id #uuid "be28023f-b4e1-527a-9fd6-a55af9912450" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35b2b5dc-be5b-531e-92b7-e1931e890d8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127596" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨0,1,1⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨0,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{({e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{({e}^{0}+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+1}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}(-⟨0,1,1⟩+2⟨0,1,0⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0(0)+1(1)+1(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00435226-3a19-5eb9-bdc3-0cc889991ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442563} {:answer/id #uuid "496f3c19-e7a7-5744-b55a-b824329dc81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442573} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1effa6-46d0-5a74-afd6-d9566c6fab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442581} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c6b0e9-05cf-5400-9c8e-e91ea5905657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442588} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a90765-83c0-5ed9-bd70-667b33af249d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}⟨-\\sqrt{6},1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f1effa6-46d0-5a74-afd6-d9566c6fab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442581} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dcaf563-74bd-51e5-bbaf-671c2387e005" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86bd8ee0-d1c5-5e4f-9534-8a4e93bb860c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127597" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(1)=⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(1)=⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6t}{{(1+3{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{6(1)}{{(1+3(1)^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot q(1)+\\frac{1}{1+3(1)^{2}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩+\\frac{1}{4}⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩+2⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{6})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{8}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{4}q(1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{6}}(1\\cdot (-3)+\\sqrt{6}\\cdot (-\\sqrt{6})+3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{6}}(-3-6+9) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9455b7e1-8e45-5b8d-8930-b09294afde81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289026} {:answer/id #uuid "39d23c9a-dade-5d1b-902c-eabbc6bdf7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39d23c9a-dade-5d1b-902c-eabbc6bdf7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442571} :answer-field/id #uuid "b860cd5e-c7b3-588c-8a3f-8511ad544821" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbe7871a-ccc9-5a95-8370-21299423ec38" :question/math-academy-id "q-222851" :question/problem "Given  the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "674f313a-77f0-5fc8-abb7-91c3e1a7184e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288758} {:answer/id #uuid "999808ca-0444-5335-ac02-8d182fdfd0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "999808ca-0444-5335-ac02-8d182fdfd0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442585} :answer-field/id #uuid "88606f6e-77e8-504e-bf1e-6d51d3bd6834" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0891613-fa7a-56c3-a9d8-1b98ea108f48" :question/math-academy-id "q-223107" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,k$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n$j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k \\\\  \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\  \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba0cef59-7cae-59d5-aee5-dae91a214aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba0cef59-7cae-59d5-aee5-dae91a214aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442594} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c25002-d570-5188-8443-f9f108b5d95d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7043d5b3-b126-54ab-a0bc-9816029b7532" :question/math-academy-id "q-223120" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$⟨\\,0\\,,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =-2⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =|-2|\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3ad161-64a2-5b71-ad24-c120e8ec6285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398741} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7e3c75-5535-5316-bea0-bf6c928449b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,j-\\frac{3}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398742} {:answer/id #uuid "6a162c78-4b2f-5cb3-bbfc-9d6ac2a6eb14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398743} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f7e9c3-d0a4-5c14-ad36-84ce7ce201f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398744} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf33e14-d217-5957-83e1-d43d4854445a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbf33e14-d217-5957-83e1-d43d4854445a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398745} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ffbf219-9fc7-55d4-a011-546b8183375e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee02912-87a3-5a99-92ba-f8ae9f270c88" :question/math-academy-id "q-33233" :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(0,3,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ) \\\\ T(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,3,4)$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,3,4⟩ \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+3\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components of $i$, $j$, and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=3 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,3,4)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k}{1}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-i)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(i\\cdot j)+\\frac{4}{5}(i\\cdot k) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e221a65-a44e-5346-8777-5df15d203193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117674} {:answer/id #uuid "3d439e47-3587-59b8-90e6-26a8e6ad90ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442572} {:answer/id #uuid "524146c4-7e77-5f6d-af6c-ffd660ac28d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442577} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c1d1e6-bb07-517c-8756-922d8ba89e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442592} {:answer/id #uuid "fbaf0be8-2245-53a4-8fe1-5686e5bfe610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "524146c4-7e77-5f6d-af6c-ffd660ac28d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442577} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aee8c82-8119-5217-bc70-4d42d2a818f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f555a45-5f75-599a-8fee-db06b9f6384d" :question/math-academy-id "q-33493" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\n \nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2})⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨0,\\,2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}[-2t⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+({t}^{2}+2)⟨0,\\,2,\\,2t⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨4t,\\,-2{t}^{2}+4,\\,4t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{((2)^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨4(2),\\,-2(2)^{2}+4,\\,4(2)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}⟨8,-4,8⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨2,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{9}‖⟨2,-1,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{9}⟨2,-1,2⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2)\\cdot T(2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}⟨-2,\\,4,\\,4⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,4,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f9b3c0-76af-5d80-92a4-5941254da04b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85f9b3c0-76af-5d80-92a4-5941254da04b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442583} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8052c5-663c-518b-bb07-10ee30f768f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f357a402-4ed9-5871-9002-ee2ada823334" :question/math-academy-id "q-358780" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{13}(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+12\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{13}(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+12\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{5}{13}‖\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{13}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{5}{13}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5871484e-21bb-5092-b09c-e08bf26d1e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321842} {:answer/id #uuid "a87dc9d9-165f-54c0-8d1c-b67b7c6e81f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a87dc9d9-165f-54c0-8d1c-b67b7c6e81f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442589} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9dd7521-e5a8-502d-93ab-04dd55f565ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77a13f7-0f57-5d23-af8b-ebbc48aa1422" :question/math-academy-id "q-358783" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b50b45b-e31f-500e-9496-cc48c7886b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442574} {:answer/id #uuid "63b699bf-e6d8-5ef0-9f70-0ede7e9e9edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442580} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b0f6c4-1c16-5380-8c5e-e758a32ebbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟨-2,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442587} {:answer/id #uuid "b10046fc-c183-59e3-ae38-7e794395db17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442591} {:answer/id #uuid "f08e5f05-4e3e-5da4-b635-cb93796cbc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63b699bf-e6d8-5ef0-9f70-0ede7e9e9edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442580} :answer-field/id #uuid "9023e46d-1d99-5286-baf3-0582b7f8b0a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7057aab9-4ddf-5b3b-9e59-0a11a291512b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74850" :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)⟩,\\,t\\ge 0 \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨t,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1))$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)⟩ & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore, $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1))$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨t,\\,1⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}})⟨t,\\,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨t,\\,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}[-⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩+({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-t⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{({1}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}‖⟨1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826649c3-ffe7-5f54-9966-eebc83615bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119235} {:answer/id #uuid "283cc10c-c7dc-5d42-98bb-ff049bacb22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442567} {:answer/id #uuid "28cf7792-7abc-5ff9-b607-d63d93877d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442568} {:answer/id #uuid "999190e0-5bbc-5bc3-8758-e312658c0541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442584} {:answer/id #uuid "bfdd62a5-1439-52d3-bd50-2dddd660304c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\,i-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826649c3-ffe7-5f54-9966-eebc83615bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119235} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e24ea7-f7a1-5490-a29b-1f9c2dc00269" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ee0cbfa-763d-5927-b2fe-7883ea79f8f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74864" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=i,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-j}{1} \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot (-j) \\\\  & =(i)\\cdot (-j) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09429f0b-5a31-576e-b53c-7a9c9089a259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442565} {:answer/id #uuid "4df4322e-2ca8-59b0-b25f-d74e9d345855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442575} {:answer/id #uuid "512116d3-97dd-54be-bc16-10567d150eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}⟨1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442576} {:answer/id #uuid "54e8dd13-f4d3-59cf-aea8-647ea53c3240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442578} {:answer/id #uuid "9b75c8af-bc89-5538-be8b-284dd95d1b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b75c8af-bc89-5538-be8b-284dd95d1b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442586} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e89c597-7b48-520a-9aa5-d249aa23a780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc713dbc-3c1f-5a9c-8a2b-b4377300bc92" :question/math-academy-id "q-75410" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨0,3⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+9)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{0}{({0}^{2}+9)\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,0⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{3}q(0)\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0+0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
